{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Docker Image.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Docker Image.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"docker image docker image","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Docker Image.md","fileName":"Docker Image.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Docker Image","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 22:09\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Containerized Microservice Architecture]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Containerized Microservice Architecture","c8andnetcore30projectsusingazure.pdf","hands-onrestfulwebserviceswithaspnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Docker Image.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Docker Image.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"docker image docker image","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Docker Image.md","fileName":"Docker Image.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Docker Image","heading":"Docker Image","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A Docker image is the packaged, versionable template used to start one or more containers. It contains the application layers and dependencies described by its Dockerfile but does not become a running process until a container is created from it.\n\nThe sales-order image is built locally, tested with `docker run`, tagged, and uploaded to a registry. Kubernetes then refers to that registered image when creating its worker pods.\n\nThe software-architecture treatment uses images to separate the versioned deployable artifact from its running container instances. Registry distribution and orchestration can then reproduce the same service package on several nodes.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Containerized Microservice Architecture","c8andnetcore30projectsusingazure.pdf","hands-onrestfulwebserviceswithaspnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF3